very and the receipt built on it verified under this repository's src/verify.js and under verify-py. Its whole text is at the end of this brief. Port it into this repository. Do not redesign the documents: spec/SPEC.md, \"A delivery, tick.delivery.v1\", is the contract, and it must not change.\n\nRead first: server.mjs, src/handlers.mjs, src/tools.mjs, src/validate.mjs, spec/SPEC.md (the work order and the delivery), test/check-delivery.test.mjs for how fixtures are made, and docs/design.md.\n\nWHAT TO BUILD\n\n1. New file src/hand-in.mjs exporting `handIn(args)`. Input: the order as `order` (its JSON text) or `order_path`, `repository` (the path of a local clone), `commit` (a sha or a name git resolves), `out` (a folder, made if missing), `agent` (a non-empty string), and optional `model` (string) and `minutes` (a finite number of zero or more). It does these in order, and each refusal is an Error in plain words that creates nothing in `out`:\n   - Read the order with the same reader read_order uses. Refuse an order that is not genuine, naming every reason.\n   - Resolve the commit in the repository. Refuse when the order's base is not an ancestor of it.\n   - List the changed files with `git diff --name-only --no-renames -z base..commit`, and refuse when any lies outside the order's territory, using the same inTerritory check_delivery uses, naming each file.\n   - The key. Folder: `process.env.TICK_ID_HOME`, else `join(homedir(), '.tick-id')`. File: `id.pem`, an Ed25519 private key in PKCS8 PEM, made once with mode 0o600 when missing, and reused after that. The key is never printed, never returned and never written to `out`.\n   - Build the delivery body: `{ order_hash: <the order's content_hash>, repository: <order.repository>, base, commit, tree, tries: [{ agent, model: model ?? null, minutes: minutes ?? null, cents: null, cost: 'unknown' }] }`.\n   - `delivery_id` is randomUUID(). `content_hash` is the document hash of the body, from src/verify.js's documentHash. The signature is over the UTF-8 bytes of `tick-delivery:v1:<delivery_id>:<content_hash>`.\n   - Write `<out>/delivery.json` with schema, alg, issued_at, `coordinator: doer:<key_id>`, delivery_id, delivery, content_hash, key, key_id, signature and limits, the limits being the three lines in the script below.\n   - Write `<out>/work.bundle`: `git update-ref refs/tick/delivery <commit>`, then `git bundle create <out>/work.bundle <base>..refs/tick/delivery`, then delete the ref. The ref is removed in a finally, also on failure.\n   - Check the written delivery with verifyDocument from src/verify.js, and throw when it is not valid.\n   - Check every path built at run time is non-empty before a command takes it, with the same nonEmpty handlers.mjs uses.\n   Answer `{ handed_in: true, delivery_id, key_id, commit, tree, folder, files: ['delivery.json', 'work.bundle'], limits }`.\n\n2. src/tools.mjs: add HAND_IN with an inputSchema (`additionalProperties: false`, the order as text or path as the other tools take it, `repository`, `commit`, `out` and `agent` required, `model` and `minutes` optional) and an outputSchema for the answer above, and add it to TOOLS.\n\n3. src/handlers.mjs: add `hand_in` to HANDLERS. Export from it whatever hand-in.mjs needs (readOrder, nonEmpty, git, inTerritory) rather than copying them.\n\n4. New file ./hand-in.mjs, the command line for an agent with no MCP client: `node hand-in.mjs <order.json> <repo> <commit> <out> [--agent NAME] [--model NAME] [--minutes N]`, with a `#!/usr/bin/env node` line. It takes the order by path. `--agent` defaults to `unknown agent`. It prints one line, `handed in <12 characters of the commit> under key <key_id>: <folder>`. Missing arguments print the usage and exit 2. A refusal prints its words to stderr and exits 1.\n\n5. Sealed checks, in check_delivery. A round's checks are sealed: the order names the suite's hash in the check's `name`, and the suite's files are not in the doer's tree, so `check_delivery` today reports a right answer as failed. A check is sealed when its name matches /\\bsuite sha256 [0-9a-f]{64}\\b/. check_delivery does not run a sealed check. It reports it with outcome `not-run`, exit_code null, duration_ms 0 and output_tail `sealed: the referee runs this after you hand in`. The verdict ignores `not-run` results, and when any exist `limits` gains a line saying how many checks are sealed and that passing here says nothing about them. With no sealed check, everything stays exactly as it is, and test/check-delivery.test.mjs must pass unchanged.\n\n6. ./package.json: add `\"tick-hand-in\": \"hand-in.mjs\"` to bin, and say in the description that an agent can hand work in.\n\n7. ./README.md: add `hand_in` to the tools, and a short section on `node hand-in.mjs` for an agent with no MCP client, saying what it needs (Node 22 and git), where the key lives, that the key never leaves the machine, and that signing proves who handed the work in, not that it is right. docs/design.md: add the tool to its list in the same voice.\n\nTESTS. New file test/hand-in.test.mjs, in the style of test/check-delivery.test.mjs: real git in a temporary folder, no network, TICK_ID_HOME set to a temporary folder, and orders signed in the test with node:crypto over `tick-work-order:v1:<order_id>:<content_hash>`. It must show:\n   - a hand-in gives a delivery verifyDocument calls valid, and changing one character of delivery.json makes it not valid;\n   - the bundle, fetched into a fresh clone of the base, gives the delivered commit and tree;\n   - no refs/tick/delivery is left behind, after success and after every refusal;\n   - two hand-ins share one key_id, and on a system that has POSIX modes the key file is 0o600;\n   - each refusal: an order with one character changed, a commit that does not descend from the base, a changed file outside the territory; each writes no delivery.json;\n   - neither the key file's text nor its base64 body appears in the answer, or in any file in `out`